1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,240
Pastry chef Nicolas Appert was looking
for a way to preserve food for longer.

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:08,720
In Brest, food prepared by his method
remained in sealed vessels

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,000
for three months at the roadstead, an anchorage
near the port.

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,320
When the Board of Health
inspected the food,

5
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:19,840
the broth, meat, beans, and green peas received
a favorable assessment.

6
00:00:19,947 --> 00:00:23,680
For the early nineteenth century, this was an
unusual result.

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,280
But preserving a few samples

8
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:29,200
and teaching other people to get the same
result were different challenges.

9
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:31,840
How could one person turn years of experiments

10
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:33,440
into a method others could use?

11
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:38,960
At the time, people preserved food
mainly with salt, sugar, and drying.

12
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:43,600
These methods extended shelf life, but changed
the food’s taste and qualities.

13
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,560
Appert, who worked as a pastry chef
and distilled spirits, looked for another way.

14
00:00:48,613 --> 00:00:53,280
He experimented with meat, broth, milk,
vegetables, and fruit.

15
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:55,440
He left prepared samples in storage,

16
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:59,280
then checked their condition later
to find out which methods had worked.

17
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:03,040
His method relied on glass bottles and
wide-mouthed vessels.

18
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,080
Appert filled them with food,
sealed them with corks,

19
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:10,080
fastened the corks with wire, and heated the
vessels in a water bath.

20
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:13,600
Different foods required different preparation
and heating times.

21
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:18,080
The shape of the neck, the quality
of the glass, and the seal all mattered.

22
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:20,160
A bottle could burst while heating.

23
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:23,760
Appert put the vessels in cloth bags to contain
the shards.

24
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,160
Green peas created another problem:

25
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:30,000
peas picked too young became too soft after
processing.

26
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,080
In March 1809, a committee

27
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:35,680
of the Society for the Encouragement
of National Industry inspected

28
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:40,160
samples prepared by Appert and gave their
condition a favorable assessment.

29
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,560
Appert reported that some foods
had been prepared

30
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:47,600
more than eight months earlier, and one sample
fifteen months before the inspection.

31
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,360
But the society had kept the vessels for only
about two months.

32
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:53,520
The committee could assess the food’s
condition,

33
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:57,440
but had not observed the entire storage period
Appert described.

34
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:02,240
The proposal to publish a detailed account
of the method came from Appert himself.

35
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:07,600
On January 30, 1810, France’s minister
of the interior sent him a letter.

36
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:09,760
It set out a reward of twelve thousand

37
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:12,720
francs on the condition that
Appert prepare and publish

38
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:17,120
a description of his method and provide
the ministry with two hundred copies.

39
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,400
The description was to be submitted to
the Board of Arts and Manufactures

40
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,000
for consideration, and
Appert had to pay for printing himself.

41
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:26,720
Appert described how to work
with different foods:

42
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,640
their preparation, the tools required, and the
steps to follow.

43
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,200
He explained the vessels, corks,

44
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:36,240
heating times, and mistakes that could affect
the result.

45
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:39,840
In 1810, Appert’s book appeared
in France,

46
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,200
turning years of individual work
into a published guide.
